        "abstract": "When the 3rd industrial era digitized industry, it began the era of data generation. Data historians on premises captured the data generated to allow simple analysis for diagnosing failures or optimization.The 4th industrial era now integrates data from on premises instrumentation (level 0) to the full enterprise (level 4). The volume of data continues to multiply as the scope of integration grows and devices yield more diagnostic and configuration information.This paper addresses the many challenges of dealing with the volume and usability of data in the 4<sup xmlns:mml=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\">th</sup> industrial era.",
